/* Unit 2: Social Psychology. Notes data and key terms for the revision site. */
window.UNIT={"id":"unit2","eyebrow":"Unit 2","title":"Social Psychology","sections":{"A":"Attribution and Person Perception","B":"Attitudes, Stereotypes and Persuasion","C":"Conformity and Obedience","D":"Prosocial Behavior"},"terms":{"A":[{"term":"Attribution","def":"The process of explaining behavior by assigning a cause to it: either something inside the person or something outside them."},{"term":"Dispositional Attribution","def":"Explaining behavior by reference to an internal quality of the person: personality, character, ability, or attitude."},{"term":"Situational Attribution","def":"Explaining behavior by reference to external circumstances: context, luck, environment, or other people."},{"term":"Fundamental Attribution Error (FAE)","def":"The tendency to overestimate dispositional causes and underestimate situational causes when explaining other people's behavior."},{"term":"Actor-Observer Bias","def":"The tendency to attribute our own behavior to the situation, and others' identical behavior to their disposition."},{"term":"Self-Serving Bias","def":"Attributing our own successes to internal factors like ability and effort, and our own failures to external factors like bad luck."},{"term":"Explanatory Style","def":"The characteristic way a person explains the causes of good and bad events, ranging from optimistic to pessimistic."},{"term":"Pessimistic Explanatory Style","def":"Explaining negative events as internal, global, and permanent. Associated with helplessness."},{"term":"Optimistic Explanatory Style","def":"Explaining negative events as external, specific, and temporary. Associated with resilience."},{"term":"Mere Exposure Effect","def":"Repeated exposure to a stimulus increases our liking of it, even without any specific positive event."},{"term":"Self-Fulfilling Prophecy","def":"A belief or expectation that alters our behavior in ways that make that belief come true."},{"term":"Social Comparison","def":"Evaluating ourselves by comparing ourselves to others. Can be upward (to someone doing better) or downward (to someone doing worse)."},{"term":"Relative Deprivation","def":"Dissatisfaction that comes from comparing our situation to a reference group, not from our absolute conditions."},{"term":"Individualistic Culture","def":"A culture in which the self is seen as independent and self-determining. The fundamental attribution error tends to be stronger here."},{"term":"Collectivist Culture","def":"A culture in which situational and relational forces are more naturally considered when explaining behavior. The fundamental attribution error tends to be weaker here."}],"B":[{"term":"Stereotype","def":"A generalized belief about a group of people. The cognitive component: a belief, not yet a feeling or an action."},{"term":"Prejudice","def":"An unjustified attitude, usually negative, toward a group or a member of it. The affective component: a feeling."},{"term":"Discrimination","def":"Treating someone differently, usually unfairly, based on their group membership. The behavioral component: an action."},{"term":"Implicit Attitude","def":"An attitude that operates below conscious awareness and can shape behavior automatically, even when someone sincerely believes they hold no such attitude."},{"term":"Just-World Phenomenon","def":"The belief that the world is fundamentally fair and people get what they deserve. Can lead to blaming victims for their own misfortune."},{"term":"Belief Perseverance","def":"The tendency to maintain a belief even after being presented with contradicting evidence, often by reframing the evidence rather than updating the belief."},{"term":"Confirmation Bias","def":"The tendency to search for, interpret, and remember information in ways that confirm what we already believe."},{"term":"Cognitive Dissonance","def":"The psychological discomfort experienced when holding two inconsistent beliefs, or when behavior contradicts a belief."},{"term":"Insufficient Justification Effect","def":"When external reward for a behavior is too small to fully explain it, people resolve the resulting dissonance by changing their attitude instead."},{"term":"Central Route (ELM)","def":"Persuasion through careful, motivated evaluation of the actual quality of arguments and evidence. Produces more durable attitude change."},{"term":"Peripheral Route (ELM)","def":"Persuasion through surface cues such as attractiveness, popularity, social proof, or source credibility, rather than argument quality. Produces less durable attitude change."},{"term":"Foot-in-the-Door Technique","def":"A compliance technique: a small request is made first, followed by a larger one, exploiting consistency pressure."},{"term":"Door-in-the-Face Technique","def":"A compliance technique: a large request expected to be refused is made first, followed by a smaller one, exploiting contrast and reciprocity."},{"term":"Low-Ball Technique","def":"A compliance technique: agreement is secured under one set of terms, then additional costs are revealed afterward, exploiting commitment."},{"term":"Out-Group Homogeneity Bias","def":"The tendency to perceive members of an out-group as more similar to each other than they actually are."},{"term":"In-Group Bias","def":"The tendency to favor members of our own group over out-group members in evaluations, resources, and attributions."},{"term":"Ethnocentrism","def":"Evaluating other cultures by the standards of one's own culture, typically concluding that one's own culture is superior or more natural."}],"C":[{"term":"Conformity","def":"Aligning one's behavior or beliefs with a peer group, without any direct command from an authority figure."},{"term":"Obedience","def":"Compliance with a direct command from a recognized authority figure."},{"term":"Normative Social Influence","def":"Conforming to gain social approval or avoid rejection, often without any change in private belief."},{"term":"Informational Social Influence","def":"Conforming because others are genuinely used as a source of accurate information, especially in an ambiguous situation."},{"term":"Asch's Line Studies","def":"Classic conformity research in which participants gave incorrect answers on about 37% of trials about visually obvious line lengths, to match a unanimous but wrong group. It demonstrated normative social influence."},{"term":"Broken Unanimity","def":"In Asch's studies, when even one confederate gave the correct answer, conformity dropped dramatically, because the group no longer looked unanimous and resistance felt legitimate."},{"term":"Milgram's Obedience Studies","def":"Classic obedience research showing that most participants would administer what they believed were dangerous shocks to another person when instructed by an authority figure."},{"term":"Legitimacy of Authority","def":"The perceived credibility and institutional standing of the person or organization giving an instruction. A key factor that increased obedience in Milgram's studies."},{"term":"Incremental Escalation","def":"Increasing demands in small steps rather than all at once. Each small step creates consistency pressure that makes the next step feel acceptable."}],"D":[{"term":"Prosocial Behavior","def":"Voluntary behavior intended to benefit others."},{"term":"Bystander Effect","def":"The finding that people are sometimes less likely to help in an emergency when more bystanders are present."},{"term":"Diffusion of Responsibility","def":"Each person's felt personal obligation to act is reduced when other bystanders are assumed to be present, because responsibility feels shared."},{"term":"Pluralistic Ignorance","def":"Each person looks to others for cues about how serious a situation is, misreads their outward calm as evidence nothing is wrong, and follows suit."},{"term":"Social Exchange Theory","def":"The view that helping behavior, even when it appears selfless, is governed by an often unconscious cost-benefit analysis."},{"term":"Social Responsibility Norm","def":"An internalized obligation to help those who depend on us, experienced as a genuine motivation rather than a calculated decision."},{"term":"Norm of Reciprocity","def":"An internalized expectation that favors should be returned, based on a history or anticipation of mutual exchange."},{"term":"Altruism","def":"Helping behavior motivated by genuine concern for another's welfare, without expectation of personal benefit."}]},"related":[["Attribution","Dispositional Attribution","Situational Attribution"],["Explanatory Style","Pessimistic Explanatory Style","Optimistic Explanatory Style"],["Social Comparison","Relative Deprivation"],["Cognitive Dissonance","Insufficient Justification Effect"],["Discrimination","In-Group Bias"],["Conformity","Normative Social Influence","Informational Social Influence"],["Asch's Line Studies","Broken Unanimity"],["Obedience","Milgram's Obedience Studies","Legitimacy of Authority"],["Incremental Escalation","Foot-in-the-Door Technique"],["Prosocial Behavior","Altruism"],["Bystander Effect","Diffusion of Responsibility","Pluralistic Ignorance"]],"mistakes":{"A":[{"think":"the fundamental attribution error and actor-observer bias are the same thing.","actually":"the FAE is about judging other people's behavior in general. Actor-observer bias is the contrast between explaining your own behavior by the situation and someone else's identical behavior by their disposition."},{"think":"self-serving bias and the FAE both just mean blaming people unfairly.","actually":"self-serving bias is about explaining your own outcomes: success inside, failure outside. The FAE is about explaining other people's behavior. They work on different targets."},{"think":"relative deprivation means someone is actually worse off than before.","actually":"it is about comparison, not absolute conditions. A person whose situation has not changed at all can feel it if their reference group changes."},{"think":"a pessimistic explanatory style just means being unhappy about a bad result.","actually":"it needs all three features together: internal, global, and permanent. 'I did not study enough for this exam' is internal but specific and temporary, so it is not pessimistic explanatory style."}],"B":[{"think":"stereotype, prejudice, and discrimination are the same thing said three ways.","actually":"a stereotype is a belief, prejudice is a feeling, and discrimination is a behavior. A scenario can show one, two, or all three."},{"think":"cognitive dissonance and belief perseverance are the same because both involve resisting change.","actually":"dissonance is discomfort that often leads to a change in attitude or behavior. Belief perseverance keeps the original belief by reframing the evidence, so nothing changes."},{"think":"the peripheral route is always manipulative and the central route is always ethical.","actually":"either route can be used honestly or dishonestly. The ethical question is about honesty and respect for the audience, not which route is used."},{"think":"foot-in-the-door and door-in-the-face are the same because both use two requests.","actually":"they differ in order and mechanism. Foot-in-the-door starts small and relies on consistency. Door-in-the-face starts big and relies on contrast and reciprocity."},{"think":"if attitude and behavior change together, the attitude change caused the behavior change.","actually":"as with any correlation, the direction of cause and possible third variables have to be considered first."}],"C":[{"think":"obedience and conformity are the same because both mean changing behavior because of others.","actually":"obedience is following a direct command from a recognized authority. Conformity is going along with a peer group without any command."},{"think":"conforming in an ambiguous situation is always normative social influence.","actually":"real uncertainty is when informational social influence is most likely, because people use others as a source of accurate information."},{"think":"Milgram found that only unusually aggressive people fully obeyed.","actually":"ordinary people obeyed at high rates, and the rates changed a lot with the situation, not with stable personality traits."},{"think":"diffusion of responsibility explains conformity in Asch's study.","actually":"diffusion of responsibility is a bystander concept about feeling less obliged to help. Asch's results are explained by normative social influence."}],"D":[{"think":"diffusion of responsibility and pluralistic ignorance are one mechanism with two names.","actually":"diffusion of responsibility is feeling less personally obliged as bystander numbers grow. Pluralistic ignorance is misreading others' calm as a sign nothing is wrong. They can happen together but work differently."},{"think":"social exchange theory proves all helping is selfish.","actually":"it is one framework among several. Anonymous helping at a personal cost, with no chance of recognition or a favor back, is hard for it to explain."},{"think":"more bystanders always means less helping.","actually":"the bystander effect is a tendency, not a rule. It depends on conditions such as ambiguity and whether responsibility feels shared."},{"think":"the bystander effect proves people are basically selfish.","actually":"most people in Darley and Latane's alone condition did help. The effect shows how the situation can suppress a strong tendency to help."}]}};
